251
00:14:34,470 --> 00:14:36,390
Silence doesn't mean failure.

252
00:14:36,390 --> 00:14:38,830
I wish I had known that earlier.

253
00:14:38,830 --> 00:14:40,530
What happened next?

254
00:14:40,530 --> 00:14:45,610
They asked me why I wanted to work for the company and my answer was very weak.

255
00:14:45,610 --> 00:14:46,930
What did you say?

256
00:14:46,930 --> 00:14:51,570
I said something like, I need a job and this position looks okay.

257
00:14:51,570 --> 00:14:55,970
That's an honest answer, but it doesn't show interest or preparation.

258
00:14:55,970 --> 00:14:59,570
Yes, I realized that immediately.

259
00:14:59,570 --> 00:15:03,610
Not researching the company is one of the biggest interview mistakes.

260
00:15:03,610 --> 00:15:06,250
I only read the job description.

261
00:15:06,250 --> 00:15:12,010
about the company's values, products, or culture can make your answer much stronger.

262
00:15:12,010 --> 00:15:16,170
I understand now. Even simple preparation could have helped.

263
00:15:16,170 --> 00:15:21,410
Exactly. You don't need to know everything, just enough to show interest.

264
00:15:21,410 --> 00:15:24,170
Another problem was my body language.

265
00:15:24,170 --> 00:15:25,410
What about it?

266
00:15:25,410 --> 00:15:28,930
I avoided eye contact and kept looking at the table.

267
00:15:28,930 --> 00:15:33,330
Body language sends strong messages, even without words.

268
00:15:33,330 --> 00:15:36,530
I didn't realize how negative that could look.

269
00:15:36,530 --> 00:15:41,210
In many business cultures, eye contact shows confidence and interest.

270
00:15:41,210 --> 00:15:45,130
I was very interested, but my body didn't show it.

271
00:15:45,130 --> 00:15:48,810
That's why practicing interviews at home is so helpful.

272
00:15:48,810 --> 00:15:52,610
I usually only practice answers in my head.

273
00:15:52,610 --> 00:15:56,010
Practicing out loud helps your body and mind work together.

274
00:15:56,010 --> 00:15:57,890
That's a good point.

275
00:15:57,890 --> 00:16:00,410
Did you ask any questions at the end of the interview?

276
00:16:00,410 --> 00:16:01,970
No, I didn't.

277
00:16:01,970 --> 00:16:04,550
My mind went completely blank.

278
00:16:04,550 --> 00:16:07,310
That's another very common mistake.

279
00:16:07,310 --> 00:16:09,670
I thought saying no was polite.

280
00:16:09,670 --> 00:16:13,950
In interviews, asking questions shows interest and motivation.

281
00:16:13,950 --> 00:16:17,310
I was afraid my English wasn't good enough.

282
00:16:17,310 --> 00:16:19,390
Simple questions are perfectly fine.

283
00:16:19,390 --> 00:16:22,310
The idea matters more than the language.

284
00:16:22,310 --> 00:16:25,430
Like asking about the team or daily tasks?

285
00:16:25,430 --> 00:16:26,430
Exactly.

286
00:16:26,430 --> 00:16:28,350
Those are excellent questions.

287
00:16:28,350 --> 00:16:31,950
I also talked negatively about my last job.

288
00:16:31,950 --> 00:16:33,530
That can be risky.

289
00:16:33,530 --> 00:16:37,370
I complained about my manager and the work environment.

290
00:16:37,370 --> 00:16:41,910
Even if it's true, it's better to speak neutrally or positively.

291
00:16:41,910 --> 00:16:45,390
So I think it made me sound unprofessional.

292
00:16:45,390 --> 00:16:49,170
Interviewers often look at how you handle difficult experiences.

293
00:16:49,170 --> 00:16:52,610
So I should focus on what I learned then instead.

294
00:16:52,610 --> 00:16:53,810
Exactly.

295
00:16:53,810 --> 00:16:57,130
Growth and learning sound much more professional.

296
00:16:57,130 --> 00:17:00,670
After talking to you, I see my mistakes more clearly.

297
00:17:00,670 --> 00:17:02,850
And that's a good thing.

298
00:17:02,850 --> 00:17:05,190
Awareness is the first step to improvement.

299
00:17:05,190 --> 00:17:08,630
I feel less embarrassed now and more motivated.

300
00:17:08,630 --> 00:17:09,790
Good.

301
00:17:09,790 --> 00:17:12,270
Every interview is a learning experience.

302
00:17:12,270 --> 00:17:15,390
You won't see the next one as something to fear.

303
00:17:15,390 --> 00:17:17,190
That's the right mindset.

304
00:17:17,190 --> 00:17:20,230
Interviews are conversations, not exams.

305
00:17:20,230 --> 00:17:22,750
Thank you for your time and advice.

306
00:17:22,750 --> 00:17:24,310
You're very welcome.

307
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:28,650
Keep practicing, keep improving, and we'll see you in the next episode.

308
00:17:28,650 --> 00:17:31,090
This is your everyday English class.

309
00:17:31,090 --> 00:17:34,210
Hello, and welcome to Business English Learning.

310
00:17:34,210 --> 00:17:35,930
Hi everyone, welcome back.

311
00:17:35,930 --> 00:17:37,250
I'm Daniel.

312
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:38,250
And I'm Sophie.

313
00:17:38,250 --> 00:17:40,690
It's great to have you with us today.

314
00:17:40,690 --> 00:17:46,730
This podcast helps you build confidence using English in real workplace situations.

315
00:17:46,730 --> 00:17:51,770
Situations where your words matter, your tone matters, and clarity is essential.

316
00:17:51,770 --> 00:17:57,490
Today's topic is very practical, participating effectively in business meetings.

317
00:17:57,490 --> 00:18:01,930
Conversations are a big challenge for many English learners, especially when the meeting

318
00:18:01,930 --> 00:18:07,210
includes native speakers who talk quickly, or when you want to share an idea but don't

319
00:18:07,210 --> 00:18:09,490
know how to enter the conversation.

320
00:18:09,490 --> 00:18:14,250
If you've ever stayed silent in a meeting even though you had good ideas, this episode

321
00:18:14,250 --> 00:18:15,250
is for you.

322
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:19,690
We'll explore this through a detailed, realistic conversation.

323
00:18:19,690 --> 00:18:24,970
So relax, listen carefully, and focus on how the speakers communicate.

324
00:18:24,970 --> 00:18:29,170
And as always, don't forget to subscribe to Business English Learning.

325
00:18:29,170 --> 00:18:31,330
Alright, let's get started.

326
00:18:31,330 --> 00:18:33,690
Hi Mark, do you have a few minutes?

327
00:18:33,690 --> 00:18:37,450
I wanted to talk about something that's been bothering me at work.

328
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:38,450
Of course, Anna.

329
00:18:38,450 --> 00:18:39,690
What's on your mind?

330
00:18:39,690 --> 00:18:43,290
I feel frustrated during team meetings.

331
00:18:43,290 --> 00:18:44,290
Frustrated how?

332
00:18:44,290 --> 00:18:49,610
I prepare ideas in advance, but when the meeting starts, I rarely speak.

333
00:18:49,610 --> 00:18:51,610
Is it because of your English?

334
00:18:51,610 --> 00:18:52,610
Yes.

335
00:18:52,610 --> 00:18:57,590
Partly, people speak fast, interrupt each other, and by the time I'm ready, the topic

336
00:18:57,590 --> 00:18:58,590
is changed.

337
00:18:58,590 --> 00:19:02,510
That's a very common problem, even for fluent speakers.

338
00:19:02,510 --> 00:19:03,510
Really?

339
00:19:03,510 --> 00:19:05,490
I thought it was just me.

340
00:19:05,490 --> 00:19:06,490
Not at all.

341
00:19:06,490 --> 00:19:08,970
Meetings can be challenging environments.

342
00:19:08,970 --> 00:19:12,550
I worry that my manager thinks I don't have ideas.

343
00:19:12,550 --> 00:19:15,290
Have you received any feedback about that?

344
00:19:15,290 --> 00:19:19,690
Not directly, but I notice others get more visibility.

345
00:19:19,690 --> 00:19:23,490
Quality is important, but it doesn't always mean speaking a lot.

346
00:19:23,490 --> 00:19:24,890
That's interesting.

347
00:19:24,890 --> 00:19:28,330
I thought speaking more was the only solution.

348
00:19:28,330 --> 00:19:31,010
Quality is more important than quantity.

349
00:19:31,010 --> 00:19:33,710
But how do I speak at the right moment?

350
00:19:33,710 --> 00:19:39,130
One useful strategy is to prepare short, clear points instead of long explanations.

351
00:19:39,130 --> 00:19:42,910
I usually prepare long answers in my head.

352
00:19:42,910 --> 00:19:45,730
That can make it harder to find the right moment.

353
00:19:45,730 --> 00:19:48,010
So shorter comments are better?

354
00:19:48,010 --> 00:19:49,010
Yes.

355
00:19:49,010 --> 00:19:54,210
For example, you can say, I'd like to add something, or can I jump in here?

356
00:19:54,210 --> 00:19:57,170
Those phrases feel polite and confident.

357
00:19:57,170 --> 00:19:58,330
Exactly.

358
00:19:58,330 --> 00:20:02,170
You don't need perfect English, just clear signals.

359
00:20:02,170 --> 00:20:03,850
Another issue is interruption.

360
00:20:03,850 --> 00:20:07,130
I start speaking and someone talks over me.

361
00:20:07,130 --> 00:20:09,490
That happens a lot in meetings.

362
00:20:09,490 --> 00:20:12,250
It makes me stop immediately.

363
00:20:12,250 --> 00:20:17,730
Instead of stopping, you can calmly continue or say, I'll just finish this point.

364
00:20:17,730 --> 00:20:21,430
That sounds confident, but also respectful.

365
00:20:21,430 --> 00:20:24,370
That's the balance you want in business communication.

366
00:20:24,370 --> 00:20:28,730
I also struggle with agreeing or disagreeing politely.

367
00:20:28,730 --> 00:20:30,230
That's an important skill.

368
00:20:30,230 --> 00:20:31,910
What do you usually do?

369
00:20:31,910 --> 00:20:34,530
If I disagree, I stay silent.

370
00:20:34,530 --> 00:20:37,290
You can disagree without sounding negative.

371
00:20:37,290 --> 00:20:38,290
How?

372
00:20:38,290 --> 00:20:43,330
For example, I see your point, but I have a slightly different perspective.

373
00:20:43,330 --> 00:20:45,530
That feels much softer.

374
00:20:45,530 --> 00:20:46,930
Exactly.

375
00:20:46,930 --> 00:20:49,990
language is very useful in meetings.

376
00:20:49,990 --> 00:20:53,130
What about when I don't understand something?

377
00:20:53,130 --> 00:20:57,050
Asking for clarification is better than pretending to understand.

378
00:20:57,050 --> 00:21:00,050
I worry it makes me look unprofessional.

379
00:21:00,050 --> 00:21:03,010
Actually, it shows responsibility.

380
00:21:03,010 --> 00:21:04,450
What could I say?

381
00:21:04,450 --> 00:21:07,010
You could say, could you clarify that point?

382
00:21:07,010 --> 00:21:10,070
Or just to confirm, do you mean...

383
00:21:10,070 --> 00:21:12,830
Those phrases sound very professional.

384
00:21:12,830 --> 00:21:16,370
They are, and they're commonly used by native speakers.

385
00:21:16,370 --> 00:21:18,310
That makes me feel more comfortable.

386
00:21:18,310 --> 00:21:19,310
Good.

387
00:21:19,310 --> 00:21:22,450
Meetings are about collaboration, not competition.

388
00:21:22,450 --> 00:21:27,370
I also feel tired after meetings because I concentrate so much.

389
00:21:27,370 --> 00:21:30,970
That's normal when you're working in a second language.

390
00:21:30,970 --> 00:21:34,730
Sometimes I feel exhausted even if I didn't speak much.

391
00:21:34,730 --> 00:21:38,810
Listening actively in another language takes a lot of energy.

392
00:21:38,810 --> 00:21:41,490
How can I improve over time?

393
00:21:41,490 --> 00:21:43,170
Review meetings afterward.

394
00:21:43,170 --> 00:21:45,770
Write down useful freezes you heard.

395
00:21:45,770 --> 00:21:48,850
So meetings can also be learning opportunities.

396
00:21:48,850 --> 00:21:50,330
Exactly.

397
00:21:50,330 --> 00:21:52,210
Every meeting helps you grow.

398
00:21:52,210 --> 00:21:54,170
I like that way of thinking.

399
00:21:54,170 --> 00:21:57,850
You can also follow up by email if you didn't get a chance to speak.

400
00:21:57,850 --> 00:22:00,050
That's a great idea.

401
00:22:00,050 --> 00:22:03,690
You can summarize your idea clearly and professionally.

402
00:22:03,690 --> 00:22:06,330
That way, my contribution is still visible.

403
00:22:06,330 --> 00:22:10,090
Yes, and managers appreciate clear follow-ups.

404
00:22:10,090 --> 00:22:13,610
After this conversation, I feel more confident about meetings.

405
00:22:13,610 --> 00:22:15,730
That's great to hear.

406
00:22:15,730 --> 00:22:18,570
Science comes from preparation and practice.

407
00:22:18,570 --> 00:22:22,010
I'll start preparing shorter points and useful phrases.

408
00:22:22,010 --> 00:22:24,750
That's an excellent plan.

409
00:22:24,750 --> 00:22:27,150
Thank you for taking the time to talk with me.

410
00:22:27,150 --> 00:22:28,150
Any time.

411
00:22:28,150 --> 00:22:30,130
You're doing better than you think.

412
00:22:30,130 --> 00:22:33,130
That was a very realistic workplace conversation.

413
00:22:33,130 --> 00:22:37,850
Yes, and many of you probably faced the same challenges in meetings.

414
00:22:37,850 --> 00:22:42,130
Remember, effective communication is not about perfect English.

415
00:22:42,130 --> 00:22:46,490
It's about clarity, confidence, and participation.

416
00:22:46,490 --> 00:22:49,610
Thank you for listening to Business English Learning.

417
00:22:49,610 --> 00:22:56,790
Keep practicing, stay confident, and we'll see you in the next episode.

418
00:22:56,790 --> 00:23:00,590
This is your Business English Learning Podcast.

419
00:23:00,590 --> 00:23:02,470
Hello and welcome, everyone.

420
00:23:02,470 --> 00:23:04,230
Hi, my name is Erica.

421
00:23:04,230 --> 00:23:05,230
And I'm Marco.

422
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:07,990
We're really happy you're here with us today.

423
00:23:07,990 --> 00:23:13,350
This podcast is made to help you practice real English in a natural and relaxed way.

424
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:19,110
Our goal is to help you feel more confident when you use English in real-life situations.

425
00:23:19,110 --> 00:23:23,770
And one of the most stressful situations for many people is a job interview.

426
00:23:23,770 --> 00:23:27,170
Even in your own language, interviews can feel scary.

427
00:23:27,170 --> 00:23:29,750
And in English, they can feel even harder.

428
00:23:29,750 --> 00:23:31,410
You might forget words.

429
00:23:31,410 --> 00:23:32,990
You might feel nervous.

430
00:23:32,990 --> 00:23:36,510
Or you might not know how to explain your ideas clearly.

431
00:23:36,510 --> 00:23:41,930
So today, we're talking about common job interview mistakes and how to avoid them.

432
00:23:41,930 --> 00:23:45,330
Don't worry if you don't understand everything the first time.

433
00:23:45,330 --> 00:23:47,210
Just listen calmly.

434
00:23:47,210 --> 00:23:49,850
Understanding comes with time and repetition.

435
00:23:49,850 --> 00:23:55,690
After the dialogue, we'll explain the vocabulary, useful phrases, and cultural tips step by

436
00:23:55,690 --> 00:23:56,850
step.

437
00:23:56,850 --> 00:24:01,290
Before we begin, don't forget to subscribe to Business English Learning so you don't

438
00:24:01,290 --> 00:24:03,050
miss future episodes.

439
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:06,490
Now take a deep breath and let's begin.

440
00:24:06,490 --> 00:24:07,490
Hi Carlos.

441
00:24:07,490 --> 00:24:08,490
Thanks for meeting me.

442
00:24:08,490 --> 00:24:11,730
I really needed to talk to someone about interviews.

443
00:24:11,730 --> 00:24:12,730
Of course, Lena.

444
00:24:12,730 --> 00:24:14,090
I'm happy to help.

445
00:24:14,090 --> 00:24:15,090
What happened?

446
00:24:15,090 --> 00:24:19,210
I had an interview last week and I feel like I did everything wrong.

447
00:24:19,210 --> 00:24:21,890
I'm sorry to hear that, but don't worry.

448
00:24:21,890 --> 00:24:24,130
Every interview is a learning experience.

449
00:24:24,130 --> 00:24:28,810
I felt nervous the whole time and my answers were very short.

450
00:24:28,810 --> 00:24:30,450
That's very common.

451
00:24:30,450 --> 00:24:32,530
What do you think was your biggest mistake?

452
00:24:32,530 --> 00:24:34,210
I didn't prepare enough.

453
00:24:34,210 --> 00:24:38,250
I only practiced answers, but I didn't study the company.

454
00:24:38,250 --> 00:24:39,890
That's a big one.

455
00:24:39,890 --> 00:24:42,690
want to see that you understand their business.

456
00:24:42,690 --> 00:24:46,810
They asked why I wanted to work there, and I said, I need a job.

457
00:24:46,810 --> 00:24:50,530
I understand your honesty, but that answer sounds weak.

458
00:24:50,530 --> 00:24:52,730
Yes, I could see their reaction.

459
00:24:52,730 --> 00:24:58,810
A better answer is something simple but thoughtful, like, I like your company's values, or

460
00:24:58,810 --> 00:25:00,930
I admire the work you do.

461
00:25:00,930 --> 00:25:04,250
So I should read their website before the interview?

462
00:25:04,250 --> 00:25:05,410
Exactly.

463
00:25:05,410 --> 00:25:08,370
Even 15 minutes makes a big difference.

464
00:25:08,370 --> 00:25:10,210
Another mistake was my appearance.

465
00:25:10,210 --> 00:25:12,450
I dressed too casually.

466
00:25:12,450 --> 00:25:14,250
First impressions matter a lot.

467
00:25:14,250 --> 00:25:16,130
I didn't look professional.

468
00:25:16,130 --> 00:25:19,810
It's always safer to dress a little more formal than too casual.

469
00:25:19,810 --> 00:25:22,010
I also arrived late.

470
00:25:22,010 --> 00:25:23,730
That's another big mistake.

471
00:25:23,730 --> 00:25:26,970
I felt stressed as soon as I walked in.

472
00:25:26,970 --> 00:25:30,530
Arriving early gives you time to calm down and focus.

473
00:25:30,530 --> 00:25:33,450
Next time, I'll try to arrive 20 minutes early.

474
00:25:33,450 --> 00:25:34,810
That's a great plan.

475
00:25:34,810 --> 00:25:37,490
During the interview, I spoke very fast.

476
00:25:37,490 --> 00:25:39,390
What happens when people are nervous?

477
00:25:39,390 --> 00:25:41,610
And my pronunciation got worse.

478
00:25:41,610 --> 00:25:43,610
The solution is simple.

479
00:25:43,610 --> 00:25:44,770
Slow down.

480
00:25:44,770 --> 00:25:47,210
I thought I had to answer immediately.

481
00:25:47,210 --> 00:25:48,210
You don't.

482
00:25:48,210 --> 00:25:49,490
Pausing is okay.

483
00:25:49,490 --> 00:25:51,690
What can I say when I need time?

484
00:25:51,690 --> 00:25:54,250
You can say, that's a good question.

485
00:25:54,250 --> 00:25:55,850
Let me think for a moment.

486
00:25:55,850 --> 00:25:57,250
That sounds professional.

487
00:25:57,250 --> 00:25:58,250
It is.

488
00:25:58,250 --> 00:26:01,810
Another mistake was talking badly about my old boss.

489
00:26:01,810 --> 00:26:04,170
That's very risky in interviews.

490
00:26:04,170 --> 00:26:05,970
I was just being honest.

491
00:26:05,970 --> 00:26:08,490
You can be honest without being negative.

492
00:26:08,490 --> 00:26:09,490
How?

493
00:26:09,490 --> 00:26:13,650
You can say, I learned a lot, but now I'm looking for new challenges.

494
00:26:13,650 --> 00:26:15,050
That sounds much better.

495
00:26:15,050 --> 00:26:17,130
Yes, it shows maturity.

496
00:26:17,130 --> 00:26:21,370
At the end, they asked if I had any questions, and I said no.

497
00:26:21,370 --> 00:26:23,290
That's another common mistake.

498
00:26:23,290 --> 00:26:24,970
I didn't know what to ask.

499
00:26:24,970 --> 00:26:28,290
You should always prepare one or two questions.

500
00:26:28,290 --> 00:26:29,290
Like what?

501
00:26:29,290 --> 00:26:33,090
You can ask, what does a normal day look like in this role?

502
00:26:33,090 --> 00:26:36,610
Or what skills are most important to succeed here?

503
00:26:36,610 --> 00:26:37,690
Those are good.

504
00:26:37,690 --> 00:26:39,770
They show interest and motivation.

505
00:26:39,770 --> 00:26:43,170
I feel better now because I understand my mistakes.

506
00:26:43,170 --> 00:26:45,370
And now you also have solutions.

507
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:48,090
Yes, I'll prepare better next time.

508
00:26:48,090 --> 00:26:51,490
You're already improving just by reflecting on this.

509
00:26:51,490 --> 00:26:53,130
Thank you for your support.

510
00:26:53,130 --> 00:26:54,130
Any time.

511
00:26:54,130 --> 00:26:55,170
Don't give up.

512
00:26:55,170 --> 00:26:57,810
That was a very realistic interview story.

513
00:26:57,810 --> 00:27:02,410
Yes, and many listeners probably felt connected to Lena's experience.

514
00:27:02,410 --> 00:27:05,590
Now let's explain what happened in the dialogue.

515
00:27:05,590 --> 00:27:08,010
First let's look at some useful phrases.

516
00:27:08,010 --> 00:27:11,470
Lena said, I feel like I did everything wrong.

517
00:27:11,470 --> 00:27:14,710
You can use this when you feel disappointed after something.

518
00:27:14,710 --> 00:27:18,970
For example, I feel like I did everything wrong in the meeting.

519
00:27:18,970 --> 00:27:22,850
Another phrase was, every interview is a learning experience.

520
00:27:22,850 --> 00:27:26,050
This means every situation teaches you something.

521
00:27:26,050 --> 00:27:29,530
You can say, every mistake is a learning experience.

522
00:27:29,530 --> 00:27:31,770
Now let's talk about vocabulary.

523
00:27:31,770 --> 00:27:34,050
The first word is interview.

524
00:27:34,050 --> 00:27:37,730
An interview is a formal conversation for a job.

525
00:27:37,730 --> 00:27:40,170
Next word, preparation.

526
00:27:40,170 --> 00:27:42,650
Preparation means getting ready before something.

527
00:27:42,650 --> 00:27:44,970
Another word, professional.

528
00:27:44,970 --> 00:27:49,090
Professional means polite, serious, and respectful at work.

529
00:27:49,090 --> 00:27:50,850
Next, confidence.

530
00:27:50,850 --> 00:27:52,970
Confidence means believing in yourself.

531
00:27:52,970 --> 00:27:54,890
And experience.

532
00:27:54,890 --> 00:27:58,330
Experience is what you learn from doing things in real life.

533
00:27:58,330 --> 00:28:00,630
Now let's talk about cultural tips.

534
00:28:00,630 --> 00:28:05,530
In many English-speaking countries, being early is better than being on time.

535
00:28:05,530 --> 00:28:07,830
It shows respect and responsibility.

536
00:28:07,830 --> 00:28:11,930
Also, it's okay to talk about your skills and interviews.

537
00:28:11,930 --> 00:28:14,170
That's not arrogance, that's clarity.

538
00:28:14,170 --> 00:28:16,410
Eye contact is important, too.

539
00:28:16,410 --> 00:28:18,850
It shows confidence and interest.

540
00:28:18,850 --> 00:28:21,890
Another cultural point, silence is okay.

541
00:28:21,890 --> 00:28:25,210
Pausing before you answer is natural and professional.

542
00:28:25,210 --> 00:28:28,230
Now, let's give you some model sentences.

543
00:28:28,230 --> 00:28:32,010
You can say, I have experience working in a team.

544
00:28:32,010 --> 00:28:35,510
You can say, I am motivated and willing to learn.

545
00:28:35,510 --> 00:28:40,930
If you're nervous, you can say, I'm a little nervous, but I'm excited about this opportunity.

546
00:28:40,930 --> 00:28:45,730
If you don't understand a question, you can say, sorry, could you repeat the question,

547
00:28:45,730 --> 00:28:46,730
please?

548
00:28:46,730 --> 00:28:50,110
If you need time, you can say, that's a good question.

549
00:28:50,110 --> 00:28:51,730
Let me think for a moment.

550
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:56,710
At the end, you can ask, what does a normal day look like in this role?

551
00:28:56,710 --> 00:29:00,230
Normal English is powerful when your ideas are clear.

552
00:29:00,230 --> 00:29:01,470
Now let's recap.

553
00:29:01,470 --> 00:29:05,030
Today you learned about common interview mistakes.

554
00:29:05,030 --> 00:29:06,790
Not preparing enough.

555
00:29:06,790 --> 00:29:07,790
Arriving late.

556
00:29:07,790 --> 00:29:09,350
Speaking negatively.

557
00:29:09,350 --> 00:29:11,390
And not asking questions.

558
00:29:11,390 --> 00:29:15,430
You also learned that interviews are conversations, not exams.

559
00:29:15,430 --> 00:29:17,510
You don't need perfect English.

560
00:29:17,510 --> 00:29:20,170
You need clear ideas and confidence.

561
00:29:20,170 --> 00:29:21,990
Now your challenge.

562
00:29:21,990 --> 00:29:27,270
For your next interview, prepare one strong answer and two questions.

563
00:29:27,270 --> 00:29:28,830
Practice them out loud.

564
00:29:28,830 --> 00:29:32,110
Small practice every day builds big confidence.

565
00:29:32,110 --> 00:29:37,530
If this episode helped you, subscribe to Business English Learning, share it with a friend,

566
00:29:37,530 --> 00:29:41,510
and keep listening, keep practicing, and keep improving.

567
00:29:41,510 --> 00:29:43,270
See you in the next episode.

568
00:29:43,270 --> 00:29:46,990
Hello and welcome to Business English Learning.

569
00:29:46,990 --> 00:29:48,430
Hi, everyone.

570
00:29:48,430 --> 00:29:49,950
My name is Kevin.

571
00:29:49,950 --> 00:29:51,870
And my name is Julia.

572
00:29:51,870 --> 00:29:59,190
This podcast helps you improve your business English in a clear, simple, and natural way.

573
00:29:59,190 --> 00:30:04,230
We know that learning English for work can be stressful, especially during important moments

574
00:30:04,230 --> 00:30:06,430
like job interviews.

575
00:30:06,430 --> 00:30:11,750
Job interviews are difficult even in your own language, and in English they can feel

576
00:30:11,750 --> 00:30:13,550
even harder.

577
00:30:13,550 --> 00:30:18,830
So today we're talking about common job interview mistakes and how to avoid them.

578
00:30:18,830 --> 00:30:25,590
Relax, listen calmly, and don't worry if you don't understand everything the first time.

579
00:30:25,590 --> 00:30:30,310
Before we start, don't forget to subscribe to Business English Learning.

580
00:30:30,310 --> 00:30:32,590
Your support helps the channel grow.

581
00:30:32,590 --> 00:30:37,110
Now, grab your coffee, relax, and let's begin.

582
00:30:37,110 --> 00:30:39,390
Hi Rachel, thanks for meeting me.

583
00:30:39,390 --> 00:30:43,790
I had a job interview yesterday, and I can't stop thinking about it.

584
00:30:43,790 --> 00:30:46,710
Of course Leo, tell me what happened.

585
00:30:46,710 --> 00:30:53,710
I felt confident at first, but when they asked unexpected questions, I became very nervous.

586
00:30:53,710 --> 00:30:55,970
That's very common.

587
00:30:55,970 --> 00:30:58,490
Unexpected questions can be challenging.

588
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:03,890
I tried to answer quickly, but my answers were messy and unclear.

589
00:31:03,890 --> 00:31:10,050
Many people think fast answers are better, but clear answers are more important.

590
00:31:10,050 --> 00:31:12,450
So it's okay to slow down?

591
00:31:12,450 --> 00:31:14,770
Yes, absolutely.

592
00:31:14,770 --> 00:31:18,690
Being a short pause helps you organize your thoughts.

593
00:31:18,690 --> 00:31:21,450
Another mistake was interrupting the interviewer.

594
00:31:21,450 --> 00:31:24,410
I didn't realize I was doing it.

595
00:31:24,410 --> 00:31:28,850
Interrupting can happen when you're nervous, but it's good to be aware of it.

596
00:31:28,850 --> 00:31:32,250
I just wanted to show that I understood the question.

597
00:31:32,250 --> 00:31:37,250
You can show understanding by listening fully and responding calmly.

598
00:31:37,250 --> 00:31:42,770
I also used very formal language, and it sounded unnatural.

599
00:31:42,770 --> 00:31:47,450
professional English is better than overly formal language.

600
00:31:47,450 --> 00:31:50,890
I think I tried too hard to sound perfect.

601
00:31:50,890 --> 00:31:53,250
That's a common mistake.

602
00:31:53,250 --> 00:31:56,090
Authentic communication is more effective.

603
00:31:56,090 --> 00:31:59,970
At the end, I forgot to thank the interviewer properly.

604
00:31:59,970 --> 00:32:05,570
A simple thank you and a polite goodbye leave a strong final impression.

605
00:32:05,570 --> 00:32:08,890
I also didn't send a follow-up email.

606
00:32:08,890 --> 00:32:12,690
Speaking up with a short thank you email is a great habit.

607
00:32:12,690 --> 00:32:14,770
I didn't know that was important.

608
00:32:14,770 --> 00:32:18,410
It shows professionalism and interest in the position.

609
00:32:18,410 --> 00:32:22,250
I feel frustrated, but I also learned a lot.

610
00:32:22,250 --> 00:32:23,930
That's a good mindset.

611
00:32:23,930 --> 00:32:26,970
Every interview is a learning experience.

612
00:32:26,970 --> 00:32:31,850
Next time I'll listen more, speak calmly, and be myself.

613
00:32:31,850 --> 00:32:34,170
That's the best approach.

614
00:32:34,170 --> 00:32:36,610
Confidence grows with experience.

615
00:32:36,610 --> 00:32:37,610
Thank you, Rachel.

616
00:32:37,610 --> 00:32:40,330
really helped me see things clearly.

617
00:32:40,330 --> 00:32:41,570
You're welcome, Leo.

618
00:32:41,570 --> 00:32:45,690
I'm sure your next interview will go much better.

619
00:32:45,690 --> 00:32:48,410
That conversation felt very realistic.

620
00:32:48,410 --> 00:32:52,970
Yes, many listeners will relate to Leo's experience.

621
00:32:52,970 --> 00:32:56,930
Now, let's explain what happened in the dialogue.

622
00:32:56,930 --> 00:33:03,010
We'll talk about useful phrases, important vocabulary, and cultural tips.

623
00:33:03,010 --> 00:33:07,450
Remember, interviews are conversations, not exams.

624
00:33:07,450 --> 00:33:09,730
You don't need perfect English.

625
00:33:09,730 --> 00:33:13,050
You need clear ideas and confidence.

626
00:33:13,050 --> 00:33:15,570
Keep practicing and keep improving.

627
00:33:15,570 --> 00:33:19,290
Thank you for listening to Business English Learning.

628
00:33:19,290 --> 00:33:20,450
See you in the next episode.
